EDICT 0012: the ban test's client address through the system's sockaddr_in on Linux

Category: 64-bit correctness, no change on Windows (P6 step 9.4).
Outside Windows and NeXT, net_dgrm.c declares its own struct in_addr for the ban test, a union whose S_addr is an
unsigned long: 8 bytes on LP64, so the union is 8-aligned, sin_addr lands at offset 8 instead of 4 and the test
reads sin_zero, which is 0. 0 & banMask equals the default banAddr 0, and every client that connects is told
"You have been banned." (found by the interop test, tools/interop.sh: the reference's server on Linux x86-64 refused
the port's client and any other). On Linux the system's headers declare the address as it is, as NeXT's branch does.
On Windows (LLP64, windows.h) nothing changes, so the oracle and the corpora are the same.

--- a/net_dgrm.c
+++ b/net_dgrm.c
@@ -25,7 +25,7 @@
 #ifdef BAN_TEST
 #if defined(_WIN32)
 #include <windows.h>
-#elif defined (NeXT)
+#elif defined (NeXT) || defined (__linux__)
 #include <sys/socket.h>
 #include <arpa/inet.h>
 #else
